9
00:00:25.265 --> 00:00:27.591
The grocery app's search box suggestions.

10
00:00:27.871 --> 00:00:42.313
As a customer types am... five suggestions appear within tens of milliseconds, ranked by what people search for, fresh with the season, free of junk, limited to what their dark store can deliver, and tuned to their own weekly staples.

11
00:00:42.593 --> 00:00:46.604
Ch 22 covered autocomplete inside a search engine.

12
00:00:46.884 --> 00:00:58.532
This chapter designs the dedicated query-suggestion system the classic interview question asks for: the data structure, the offline pipeline, the serving tier and personalisation.

13
00:00:58.812 --> 00:01:00.583
L1: the data structure.

14
00:01:00.863 --> 00:01:03.593
Over 200,000 suggestion candidates:

15
00:01:04.943 --> 00:01:08.186
Scanning took 2.8 ms p50 per keystroke.

16
00:01:08.466 --> 00:01:22.098
A trie with a subtree search took 108 µs p50 but 3.1 ms p99, because short prefixes like m have 27,162 candidates below them.

17
00:01:22.378 --> 00:01:36.714
A trie with the top-5 precomputed at every node answered in 0.2 µs p50 and 2.2 µs p99, with identical results, for 24 MB and a 1.2 s build.

18
00:01:36.994 --> 00:01:39.554
L2: from query logs to suggestions.

19
00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:41.903
Over 30 days of synthetic logs:

20
00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:49.309
All-time counts surfaced the mango season 11 days late and kept suggesting rakhi on every day after the festival.

21
00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:54.733
A 2-day half-life with a trending boost caught mango on day 17.

22
00:01:55.013 --> 00:01:59.493
An event calendar showed rakhi on all 4 festival days and 0 after.

23
00:01:59.773 --> 00:02:09.032
Without a filtering stage, phone numbers, a slur, a misspelling and zero-result queries were suggested on 96 prefix-days by every method.

24
00:02:09.312 --> 00:02:11.296
L3: keystroke economics.

25
00:02:11.576 --> 00:02:13.347
20,000 typing sessions:

26
00:02:13.627 --> 00:02:21.563
Rendering every response produced 41.8 stale jump-backs per 100 searches; sequence numbers made that 0.

27
00:02:22.913 --> 00:02:30.166
A 120 ms debounce cut origin requests only 17%, since most people type slower than that.

28
00:02:30.446 --> 00:02:46.105
Edge caching of 1 to 3-character prefixes plus client reuse of complete lists cut origin requests from 5.82 to 3.12 per search and the p95 from 479 to 308 ms.

29
00:02:46.385 --> 00:02:49.030
L4: personalisation and availability.

30
00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.379
For 5,000 repeat customers:

31
00:02:51.659 --> 00:03:02.091
Global popularity put the wanted item in the top 5 after one character 16% of the time, and 15% of suggestions weren't stocked in the customer's store.

32
00:03:02.371 --> 00:03:06.553
Filtering by store brought unstocked suggestions to 0%.

33
00:03:07.903 --> 00:03:16.969
Blending the customer's own history reached 73% at one character, and keystrokes to find dropped from 11.6 to 4.2.

34
00:03:17.249 --> 00:03:18.231
Reading time:

35
00:03:18.511 --> 00:03:19.983
90 min + code.

36
00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:22.186
Part 1.

37
00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:24.386
Introduction and Why It Matters.

38
00:03:25.636 --> 00:03:28.153
A story: suggestions that embarrassed us.

39
00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:35.132
The first grocery autocomplete ran a LIKE 'prefix%' query against the product table, ordered by sales.

40
00:03:35.412 --> 00:03:38.932
It was replaced by a nightly job that counted search queries.

41
00:03:39.212 --> 00:03:40.151
Within a month:

42
00:03:40.431 --> 00:03:51.545
It was slow on the first letters. a and m returned hundreds of thousands of candidates; p99 was over 300 ms, and the suggestions lagged behind typing.

43
00:03:51.825 --> 00:03:53.596
It showed a customer's phone number.

44
00:03:53.876 --> 00:03:59.785
People type call 98450xxxxx into search boxes.

45
00:04:00.065 --> 00:04:02.668
A popular one became a suggestion for c.

46
00:04:04.018 --> 00:04:09.117
It missed the mango season for two weeks, and kept suggesting rakhi until September.

47
00:04:09.397 --> 00:04:10.634
It jumped backwards.

48
00:04:10.914 --> 00:04:15.607
A slow response for a arrived after the response for amul b and replaced it.

49
00:04:15.887 --> 00:04:18.170
It suggested things the store couldn't deliver.

50
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.333
Alphonso mango was top of the list in a dark store that had none.

51
00:04:22.613 --> 00:04:24.042
It ignored the customer.

52
00:04:24.322 --> 00:04:30.871
A family that bought the same toned milk every Monday still had to type amul taaza toned 1l in full.

53
00:04:32.221 --> 00:04:42.739
A good autocomplete saves keystrokes, steers people to queries that return results, and is one of the highest-traffic endpoints in the app: every keystroke is a request.

54
00:04:43.019 --> 00:04:44.747
This chapter builds it properly.

55
00:04:46.097 --> 00:04:46.779
Quote:

56
00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:48.723
The thesis of this chapter.

57
00:04:49.003 --> 00:04:52.971
Autocomplete is a read-optimised, precomputed ranking problem.

58
00:04:53.251 --> 00:04:57.049
At query time there's only time to walk to a prefix and read a list.

59
00:04:57.329 --> 00:05:08.742
So the work moves offline: the data structure precomputes each prefix's top-k (L1); the pipeline turns noisy logs into clean, fresh, time-aware counts (L2).

60
00:05:09.022 --> 00:05:16.233
The serving tier respects keystroke economics, sequence numbers, debouncing, edge and client caches (L3).

61
00:05:16.513 --> 00:05:21.825
And the final ranking is re-ranked per request for the customer's store and history (L4).

62
00:05:22.105 --> 00:05:27.246
What you suggest is also an editorial and privacy decision: filter before you rank.

63
00:05:28.596 --> 00:05:30.900
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

