205
00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:42.165
Tag requests with a sequence number, and render only responses newer than what's displayed.

206
00:15:42.445 --> 00:15:44.706
Also cancel superseded requests.

207
00:15:44.986 --> 00:15:47.119
Debounce trades latency for traffic.

208
00:15:47.399 --> 00:15:52.199
With typing slower than the debounce window, it saves little (17% here).

209
00:15:52.479 --> 00:15:56.298
Tune it on real inter-key times, or skip it on fast networks.

210
00:15:56.578 --> 00:15:58.925
Edge cache the first 1 to 3 characters.

211
00:15:59.205 --> 00:16:04.879
There are only a few thousand such prefixes; cache them per city or language with a short TTL.

212
00:16:05.159 --> 00:16:11.261
Client cache: if a prefix returned fewer than k results, every longer prefix is a local filter.

213
00:16:11.541 --> 00:16:14.271
Also cache recent responses for backspacing.

214
00:16:15.621 --> 00:16:23.301
Payloads: tiny JSON, gzip, HTTP or 2 keep-alive; the server does an in-memory lookup and nothing else.

215
00:16:23.581 --> 00:16:28.275
Sharding is usually unnecessary: replicate the full index to every node.

216
00:16:28.555 --> 00:16:40.885
If it's too big (many cities times languages times personal data), shard by city or language, not by first letter, letter ranges are badly unbalanced, because m and a are far hotter than x.

217
00:16:41.165 --> 00:16:48.461
Hash the prefix when sharding within a city, and keep the hottest short prefixes in the edge cache anyway (Ch 11).

