442
00:38:43.213 --> 00:38:44.386
Filtering by the.

443
00:38:44.666 --> 00:38:46.181
The rest is in the chapter.

444
00:38:49.031 --> 00:38:49.884
Reading it:

445
00:38:50.164 --> 00:39:00.425
Global popularity finds the wanted item after one character only 16% of the time, and 15% of what it suggests isn't in the customer's store.

446
00:39:00.705 --> 00:39:09.260
Availability filtering removes all of those, and loses ~2 points of recall on items the customer wanted but couldn't have had delivered.

447
00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:10.841
That's the right trade.

448
00:39:11.121 --> 00:39:21.788
Personal history puts the wanted item in the top 5 after one character 73% of the time, and cuts keystrokes to find from 11.6 to 4.2.

449
00:39:22.068 --> 00:39:26.740
It's a re-rank of global candidates plus a small per-user list, not a new index.

450
00:39:28.090 --> 00:39:34.362
The lab takes about a minute; the prefix lookup is a sorted-list range scan kept simple for readability.

451
00:39:35.712 --> 00:39:36.651
Part 6.

452
00:39:36.931 --> 00:39:38.211
Production Concerns.

453
00:39:39.461 --> 00:39:40.613
Failure modes.

454
00:39:40.893 --> 00:39:42.237
Here is a quick table:

455
00:39:42.517 --> 00:39:44.799
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

456
00:39:45.079 --> 00:39:47.085
Subtree search on short prefixes.

457
00:39:47.365 --> 00:39:48.175
Symptom:

458
00:39:48.455 --> 00:39:51.485
High p99 on first keystrokes (L1).

459
00:39:51.765 --> 00:39:52.767
Mitigation:

460
00:39:53.047 --> 00:39:54.498
Precomputed top-k.

461
00:39:54.778 --> 00:39:55.802
Stale counts.

462
00:39:56.082 --> 00:39:56.893
Symptom:

463
00:39:57.173 --> 00:40:00.330
Seasonal and event terms late or lingering (L2).

464
00:40:00.610 --> 00:40:01.613
Mitigation:

465
00:40:01.893 --> 00:40:03.898
Decay, trending, event calendar.

466
00:40:04.178 --> 00:40:05.245
Junk in logs.

467
00:40:05.525 --> 00:40:06.335
Symptom:

468
00:40:06.615 --> 00:40:09.901
PII, slurs, zero-result suggestions (L2).

469
00:40:10.181 --> 00:40:11.183
Mitigation:

470
00:40:11.463 --> 00:40:14.087
Filtering stage, thresholds, overrides.

471
00:40:14.367 --> 00:40:15.839
Out-of-order responses.

472
00:40:16.119 --> 00:40:16.930
Symptom:

473
00:40:17.210 --> 00:40:19.514
Suggestions jump backwards (L3).

474
00:40:19.794 --> 00:40:20.797
Mitigation:

475
00:40:21.077 --> 00:40:23.039
Sequence numbers, cancellation.

476
00:40:23.319 --> 00:40:24.557
Keystroke storms.

477
00:40:24.837 --> 00:40:25.647
Symptom:

478
00:40:25.927 --> 00:40:27.229
Origin overload.

479
00:40:27.509 --> 00:40:28.511
Mitigation:

480
00:40:28.791 --> 00:40:31.394
Edge cache, client cache, debounce (L3).

481
00:40:31.674 --> 00:40:32.975
Unavailable items.

482
00:40:33.255 --> 00:40:34.066
Symptom:

483
00:40:34.346 --> 00:40:36.437
Out of stock after tapping (L4).

484
00:40:36.717 --> 00:40:37.719
Mitigation:

485
00:40:37.999 --> 00:40:39.642
Store availability filter.

486
00:40:40.992 --> 00:40:42.208
Bad index build.

487
00:40:42.488 --> 00:40:43.299
Symptom:

488
00:40:43.579 --> 00:40:45.285
Wrong suggestions everywhere.

489
00:40:45.565 --> 00:40:46.568
Mitigation:

490
00:40:46.848 --> 00:40:49.920
Validation, versioned artefacts, instant rollback.

491
00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:52.358
Observability.

492
00:40:52.638 --> 00:40:56.713
Server latency and QPS per prefix length; edge hit ratio.

493
00:40:56.993 --> 00:41:02.795
Suggestion CTR, keystrokes per search, zero-result rate after tapping a suggestion.

494
00:41:03.075 --> 00:41:08.814
New suggestions per build (a spike means a pipeline problem); blocked-term exposure alarms.

495
00:41:09.094 --> 00:41:13.169
Index version per node; build durations and validation results.

496
00:41:14.519 --> 00:41:16.076
Security and privacy.

497
00:41:16.356 --> 00:41:22.500
No personal data in global suggestions: thresholds on distinct users, PII filters.

498
00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:28.156
Personal history is per user and deletable (erasure requests remove it immediately).

499
00:41:28.436 --> 00:41:32.511
Rate-limit suggestion endpoints against scraping (Ch 39).

500
00:41:33.861 --> 00:41:34.757
Performance.

501
00:41:35.037 --> 00:41:41.949
Everything in memory; avoid allocation per request (pre-built response bytes per node for the hottest prefixes).

502
00:41:42.229 --> 00:41:46.943
Keep payloads under 1 KB; HTTP or 2; edge close to users.

503
00:41:47.223 --> 00:41:49.591
Warm new index versions before swapping.

504
00:41:50.941 --> 00:41:51.667
Cost.

505
00:41:51.947 --> 00:41:56.085
Tiny compute per request; the edge absorbs the hottest prefixes.

506
00:41:56.365 --> 00:42:02.381
Memory scales with cities times languages; compress tries and trim top-k lists on cold nodes.

507
00:42:02.661 --> 00:42:06.523
The offline pipeline is a small batch job (Ch 35).

508
00:42:07.873 --> 00:42:08.811
Part 7.

509
00:42:09.091 --> 00:42:10.798
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

510
00:42:12.048 --> 00:42:12.965
Watch out:

511
00:42:13.245 --> 00:42:13.885
1.

512
00:42:14.165 --> 00:42:16.576
LIKE 'prefix%' on the product table.

513
00:42:16.856 --> 00:42:19.971
Slow on short prefixes and ranks by the wrong thing.

514
00:42:21.321 --> 00:42:22.238
Watch out:

515
00:42:22.518 --> 00:42:23.222
2.

516
00:42:23.502 --> 00:42:25.187
Subtree search at query time.

517
00:42:25.467 --> 00:42:29.926
Short prefixes are both the most common and the most expensive (L1).

518
00:42:31.276 --> 00:42:32.193
Watch out:

519
00:42:32.473 --> 00:42:33.220
3.

520
00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:34.652
All-time counts.

521
00:42:34.932 --> 00:42:37.791
Late for seasons, stale after events (L2).

522
00:42:39.141 --> 00:42:40.058
Watch out:

523
00:42:40.338 --> 00:42:41.042
4.

524
00:42:41.322 --> 00:42:42.751
No filtering stage.

525
00:42:43.031 --> 00:42:46.231
Phone numbers and slurs become suggestions (L2).

526
00:42:47.581 --> 00:42:48.499
Watch out:

527
00:42:48.779 --> 00:42:49.547
5.

528
00:42:49.827 --> 00:42:51.853
Suggesting zero-result queries.

529
00:42:52.133 --> 00:42:54.181
Leads customers to empty pages.

530
00:42:55.531 --> 00:42:56.449
Watch out:

531
00:42:56.729 --> 00:42:57.475
6.

532
00:42:57.755 --> 00:42:59.398
Rendering every response.

533
00:42:59.678 --> 00:43:02.579
Out-of-order responses jump backwards (L3).

534
00:43:03.929 --> 00:43:04.847
Watch out:

535
00:43:05.127 --> 00:43:05.873
7.

536
00:43:06.153 --> 00:43:08.415
Debounce as the only traffic control.

537
00:43:08.695 --> 00:43:11.169
Saves little and adds latency (L3).

538
00:43:12.519 --> 00:43:13.437
Watch out:

539
00:43:13.717 --> 00:43:14.399
8.

540
00:43:14.679 --> 00:43:18.093
Sharding by first letter. m and a are far hotter than x.

541
00:43:19.443 --> 00:43:20.360
Watch out:

542
00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:21.387
9.

543
00:43:21.667 --> 00:43:23.160
Ignoring availability.

544
00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:26.000
Suggestions that end in out of stock (L4).

545
00:43:27.350 --> 00:43:28.267
Watch out:

546
00:43:28.547 --> 00:43:29.251
10.

547
00:43:29.531 --> 00:43:30.854
Per-user indexes.

548
00:43:31.134 --> 00:43:33.950
Personalise by re-ranking global candidates.

549
00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:36.217
Watch out:

550
00:43:36.497 --> 00:43:37.329
11.

551
00:43:37.609 --> 00:43:40.127
In-place count updates in a precomputed trie.

552
00:43:40.407 --> 00:43:42.007
Rebuild offline and swap.

553
00:43:43.357 --> 00:43:44.274
Watch out:

554
00:43:44.554 --> 00:43:45.343
12.

555
00:43:45.623 --> 00:43:46.882
No override path.

556
00:43:47.162 --> 00:43:50.447
Fixing a bad suggestion shouldn't wait for tonight's build.

557
00:43:51.797 --> 00:43:52.672
Part 8.

558
00:43:52.952 --> 00:43:54.296
Interview Q and A.

559
00:43:55.546 --> 00:43:56.378
Beginner.

560
00:43:56.658 --> 00:43:57.682
Question 1.

561
00:43:57.962 --> 00:44:00.522
Design search autocomplete, the core idea?

562
00:44:00.802 --> 00:44:02.167
Thirty-second answer:

563
00:44:02.447 --> 00:44:09.701
Build a trie of candidate queries from search logs, with the top-k most popular completions precomputed at every node.

564
00:44:09.981 --> 00:44:13.586
On each keystroke, walk to the prefix node and return its list.

565
00:44:13.866 --> 00:44:20.479
Build offline, serve from memory, cache short prefixes at the edge, and update daily plus hourly for trending.

566
00:44:21.829 --> 00:44:22.832
Question 2.

567
00:44:23.112 --> 00:44:25.629
Why precompute top-k at every trie node?

568
00:44:25.909 --> 00:44:27.275
Thirty-second answer:

569
00:44:27.555 --> 00:44:35.533
Short prefixes have enormous subtrees, m had 27,162 candidates, and they're typed on every search.

570
00:44:35.813 --> 00:44:44.155
Precomputing moves the ranking to build time, so each request is a walk plus a read of k items, independent of subtree size.

571
00:44:45.505 --> 00:44:46.550
Question 3.

572
00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:48.537
Where do suggestions come from?

573
00:44:48.817 --> 00:44:50.182
Thirty-second answer:

574
00:44:50.462 --> 00:45:02.174
Search logs (submitted queries, weighted by success, results returned, clicks, add-to-cart), plus the product catalogue (names, brands, categories) for coverage, plus the user's own history.

575
00:45:02.454 --> 00:45:07.489
Logs are normalised, filtered and scored with time decay before building the index.

576
00:45:08.839 --> 00:45:09.905
Question 4.

577
00:45:10.185 --> 00:45:12.383
How do you estimate autocomplete load?

578
00:45:12.663 --> 00:45:14.028
Thirty-second answer:

579
00:45:14.308 --> 00:45:22.073
Searches per second at peak times keystrokes per search (~6), then subtract what caching and client behaviour remove.

580
00:45:22.353 --> 00:45:36.412
330 searches per second times 5.8 about 1,900 req per second, with bursts several times higher; edge and client caching cut origin traffic to ~3 per search (L3).

581
00:45:37.762 --> 00:45:38.765
Intermediate.

582
00:45:39.045 --> 00:45:40.154
Question 5.

583
00:45:40.434 --> 00:45:43.741
How do you keep suggestions fresh without losing stability?

584
00:45:44.021 --> 00:45:45.386
Thirty-second answer:

585
00:45:45.666 --> 00:46:02.946
Score with exponential time decay (a short half-life, for example, 2 days), add a trending boost when recent volume exceeds the trailing average, rebuild nightly with an hourly trending overlay, and use a merchandising calendar for known events, pinned during, removed after.

586
00:46:04.296 --> 00:46:05.405
Question 6.

587
00:46:05.685 --> 00:46:07.627
How do you stop bad suggestions?

588
00:46:07.907 --> 00:46:09.272
Thirty-second answer:

589
00:46:09.552 --> 00:46:20.240
A filtering stage in the pipeline: minimum distinct users, blocklists, PII detection on normalised text, and removing queries that return zero results.

590
00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:28.371
Plus build-time validation, a daily review of new suggestions, and serving-time overrides to suppress anything immediately.

591
00:46:29.721 --> 00:46:30.809
Question 7.

592
00:46:31.089 --> 00:46:32.390
What should the client do?

593
00:46:32.670 --> 00:46:34.035
Thirty-second answer:

594
00:46:34.315 --> 00:46:53.302
Tag requests with a sequence number and render only newer responses; cancel superseded requests; cache recent prefix responses; reuse a complete list (fewer than k results) for longer prefixes locally; debounce lightly on slow networks; show cached suggestions instantly on backspace.

595
00:46:54.652 --> 00:46:55.676
Question 8.

596
00:46:55.956 --> 00:46:57.663
How would you cache autocomplete?

597
00:46:57.943 --> 00:46:59.308
Thirty-second answer:

598
00:46:59.588 --> 00:47:09.167
At the edge or CDN for short prefixes (1 to 3 chars), keyed by city and language, with a TTL of minutes, few keys, very high hit rate.

599
00:47:09.447 --> 00:47:12.028
In the service, the index is already in memory.

600
00:47:12.308 --> 00:47:17.001
On the client, a small LRU of recent prefixes and complete-list reuse.

601
00:47:17.281 --> 00:47:21.356
Personalised re-ranking happens after the cached global candidates.

602
00:47:22.706 --> 00:47:23.773
Question 9.

603
00:47:24.053 --> 00:47:26.719
Trie versus search-engine completion suggester?

604
00:47:26.999 --> 00:47:28.365
Thirty-second answer:

605
00:47:28.645 --> 00:47:42.362
Search engines offer FST-based completion suggesters and edge n-gram fields (Ch 22), which are good when suggestions are documents in the index and you want engine features (fuzzy, contexts).

606
00:47:42.642 --> 00:47:50.237
A dedicated in-memory trie service gives the lowest latency and full control over ranking, pipelines and overrides.

607
00:47:50.517 --> 00:47:54.805
Many teams start with the engine and move to a dedicated service at scale.

608
00:47:56.155 --> 00:47:56.923
Senior.

609
00:47:57.203 --> 00:47:58.248
Question 10.

610
00:47:58.528 --> 00:48:00.512
How do you personalise suggestions?

611
00:48:00.792 --> 00:48:02.157
Thirty-second answer:

612
00:48:02.437 --> 00:48:15.771
Fetch global candidates for the prefix (top 20 to 50), then re-rank per request with the user's history (recent searches and purchases from a KV store) and constraints (store availability, language).

613
00:48:16.051 --> 00:48:22.323
Blend scores, for example, global share + w times personal share, tuned by A or B tests.

614
00:48:23.673 --> 00:48:24.803
Question 11.

615
00:48:25.083 --> 00:48:27.131
How do you handle store availability?

616
00:48:27.411 --> 00:48:28.777
Thirty-second answer:

617
00:48:29.057 --> 00:48:40.022
Suggestions that map to products are filtered or demoted by the customer's dark-store availability at request time, using per-store bitmaps updated from the inventory stream.

618
00:48:40.302 --> 00:48:42.222
Generic queries (milk) remain.

619
00:48:42.502 --> 00:48:46.107
Fetch more candidates than you show so filtering still leaves k.

620
00:48:47.457 --> 00:48:48.545
Question 12.

621
00:48:48.825 --> 00:48:52.217
How would you shard the autocomplete index if it doesn't fit?

622
00:48:52.497 --> 00:48:53.863
Thirty-second answer:

623
00:48:54.143 --> 00:48:57.812
First by city and language, since each request is for one of them.

624
00:48:58.092 --> 00:49:13.601
If a single index is still too big, hash by prefix (or by the first n characters with care) rather than by alphabetical range, because letter ranges are heavily skewed; keep the hottest short prefixes at the edge; replicate each shard for reads.

625
00:49:14.951 --> 00:49:16.231
Question 13.

626
00:49:16.511 --> 00:49:18.282
How do you add spelling tolerance?

627
00:49:18.562 --> 00:49:19.927
Thirty-second answer:

628
00:49:20.207 --> 00:49:35.567
Normalise first (case, scripts, transliteration); then for prefixes longer than ~3 characters with few exact results, search the trie with a bounded edit distance (Levenshtein automaton) and blend fuzzy results with a penalty.

629
00:49:35.847 --> 00:49:40.711
Learn common misspellings from logs (mlik, then milk) as explicit aliases.

630
00:49:42.061 --> 00:49:43.256
Question 14.

631
00:49:43.536 --> 00:49:45.499
How do you roll out a new index safely?

632
00:49:45.779 --> 00:49:47.144
Thirty-second answer:

633
00:49:47.424 --> 00:50:01.568
Build offline, validate (golden prefixes, blocklist tests, size and diff thresholds), publish a versioned artefact, canary to a few nodes, compare metrics, then roll out; keep the previous version for instant rollback.

634
00:50:01.848 --> 00:50:05.795
Serving-time overrides handle urgent fixes without a rebuild.

635
00:50:07.145 --> 00:50:08.041
Architect.

636
00:50:08.321 --> 00:50:09.515
Question 15.

637
00:50:09.795 --> 00:50:14.617
Design autocomplete for a multi-city, multilingual grocery app end to end.

638
00:50:14.897 --> 00:50:16.262
Thirty-second answer:

639
00:50:16.542 --> 00:50:22.985
Logs to Kafka; a nightly pipeline normalises, filters, decays and scores per city and language,

640
00:50:23.265 --> 00:50:45.835
with hourly trending and an event calendar; a builder precomputes top-30 per node and validates; versioned artefacts are hot-swapped into in-memory services; edge caches for short prefixes; clients with sequence numbers and caches; request-time re-ranking by store availability and user history; overrides; evaluation on keystrokes saved and conversion.

641
00:50:47.185 --> 00:50:48.380
Question 16.

642
00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:50.985
How do you evaluate an autocomplete change?

643
00:50:51.265 --> 00:50:52.631
Thirty-second answer:

644
00:50:52.911 --> 00:51:01.252
Offline on held-out sessions: keystrokes saved, success@k by prefix length, MRR, zero-result rate of suggestions.

645
00:51:01.532 --> 00:51:08.145
Online A or B: suggestion CTR, searches per session, search-to-cart, abandonment, latency.

646
00:51:08.425 --> 00:51:11.967
Guardrails: blocked exposure, unavailable suggestions.

647
00:51:13.317 --> 00:51:14.661
Question 17.

648
00:51:14.941 --> 00:51:17.181
Real-time trending, how would you build it?

649
00:51:17.461 --> 00:51:18.826
Thirty-second answer:

650
00:51:19.106 --> 00:51:39.074
Stream search events through a windowed aggregation (Flink or Kafka Streams) computing counts per normalised query over the last hour versus a baseline; apply the same filters; emit a small overlay list per city or language every few minutes; merge it at serving time with the nightly index, capped and thresholded.

651
00:51:40.424 --> 00:51:41.597
Question 18.

652
00:51:41.877 --> 00:51:43.819
What are the privacy considerations?

653
00:51:44.099 --> 00:51:45.464
Thirty-second answer:

654
00:51:45.744 --> 00:51:54.021
Global suggestions must never reveal an individual's queries: thresholds on distinct users, PII filters, and aggregation.

655
00:51:54.301 --> 00:52:03.261
Personal history is visible only to its owner, deletable, retained for a bounded period, and excluded on shared or logged-out devices.

656
00:52:03.541 --> 00:52:05.973
Log retention and purpose limits apply.

657
00:52:07.323 --> 00:52:08.561
Question 19.

658
00:52:08.841 --> 00:52:10.547
The index doubles every year.

659
00:52:10.827 --> 00:52:11.873
What changes?

660
00:52:12.153 --> 00:52:13.518
Thirty-second answer:

661
00:52:13.798 --> 00:52:31.398
Compress (radix trie or FST), trim top-k on cold nodes, split per city or language, move long-tail suggestions to a secondary tier (engine-backed) queried only when the in-memory tier has few results, and keep the edge doing the heavy lifting for short prefixes.

662
00:52:32.748 --> 00:52:33.857
Question 20.

663
00:52:34.137 --> 00:52:36.868
How does autocomplete connect to the rest of search?

664
00:52:37.148 --> 00:52:38.513
Thirty-second answer:

665
00:52:38.793 --> 00:52:58.868
It shares normalisation, synonyms and transliteration with the search engine (Ch 22), uses the engine to check that suggestions return results, feeds search logs back into its own pipeline, and passes the chosen suggestion (with a suggested flag) to search so ranking and analytics can attribute it.

666
00:53:00.218 --> 00:53:01.114
Part 9.

667
00:53:01.394 --> 00:53:02.802
Summary and Practice.

668
00:53:04.052 --> 00:53:05.140
Key takeaways.

669
00:53:05.420 --> 00:53:11.287
Precompute top-k per trie node; short prefixes make query-time search expensive (L1).

670
00:53:11.567 --> 00:53:15.983
Clean, decayed, trending counts, plus a calendar for known events (L2).

671
00:53:16.263 --> 00:53:21.340
Filtering is part of ranking: thresholds, blocklists, PII, zero results (L2).

672
